Start an outbound call from a specific Aircall user with `POST /v1/users/:id/calls`.
Use this endpoint when your integration already knows which Aircall user should place the call and which Aircall number they should call from.

Before you send the request:
1. Create or use an existing Aircall account. [Sign up as a customer](https://aircall.io/get-started/) or, [if you qualify to be a tech partner](/docs/how-to-publish-an-app#requirements), [sign up for a developer account](https://aircall.io/partners/technology/).
2. Set up authentication with [OAuth](/docs/oauth) or [Basic Auth](/docs/basic-authentication). This guide uses Basic Auth.
3. Install [Aircall Workspace](https://aircall.io/download/) for the user who will place the call.
4. Get the Aircall user ID for the agent who will place the call. You can fetch it with `GET /v1/users` or find it in the Aircall Dashboard.
5. Get the Aircall number ID to call from. The user must be assigned to this number. You can fetch the user details with `GET /v1/users/:id` and check the `numbers` array.
6. Format the destination phone number in E.164 format, for example `+18001231234`.
##
Send the outbound call request
The `POST /v1/users/:id/calls` endpoint starts the outbound call automatically. It requires the Aircall user ID in the path and `number_id` plus `to` in the JSON body.
| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `id` | integer | Yes | Aircall user ID for the user who should place the call. |
| `number_id` | integer | Yes | Aircall number ID to use as the outbound caller number. The user must be assigned to this number. |
| `to` | string | Yes | Destination phone number in E.164 format, for example `+18001231234`. |
A successful request returns `204 No Content`. Do not parse a response body from the Aircall API when the call starts successfully.

